4101: no-crossing marker; from discovery routing it reads \(D\) exactly for discovery
4102: claims; and from the evidence-level definition it reads \(B\), either the
4103: path-verified boundary or the five command-run fields.  These are finite fields
4104: on the submitted surface, so the constructor is deterministic and partial only
4105: when a required field is absent.  By
4106: Theorem~\ref{thm:record-acceptance-exactness}, acceptance of the constructed tuple
4107: is equivalent to \(\mathsf{Acc}_{\Gamma}\), proving the first item.
4108: 
4109: For rejection, run the predicates \(P_1,\ldots,P_6\) of
4110: Theorem~\ref{thm:verifier-input-output-contract} in order and return the finite
4111: fields read by the first false predicate.  If the failure is not merely an
4112: out-of-domain kind or level, it is one of the five substantive coordinates
4113: \(E,R,H,D,B\).  The exhaustion argument of
4114: Theorem~\ref{thm:finite-certificate-obstruction-skeleton} proves that these are
4115: exactly the five listed obstruction skeletons, and deleting unread coordinates
4116: gives inclusion-minimality.  If the failure is an out-of-domain kind or level,
4117: the same theorem reduces it to the corresponding assigned-gate or evidence-level
4118: boundary obstruction, since \(K\) determines the finite gate set and \(\ell\)
4119: determines the boundary obligation.
4120: 
4121: Finally let \(c^{+}\) be a proper stronger reading of an accepted row.  By
4122: Theorem~\ref{thm:finite-extension-budget-normal-form}, such a reading changes at
4123: least one finite coordinate unless it is record-equivalent to \(c\).  The first
4124: changed coordinate not supplied on the submitted support surface is read by one
4125: of the six predicates and is therefore returned by the extractor.  If all changed
4126: coordinates are supplied and pass their assigned gates, the enlarged tuple is a
4127: new accepted certificate for the enlarged claim; if none are changed, the finite
4128: preorder has not distinguished \(c^{+}\) from \(c\).\end{proof}
4129: 
4130: \begin{proposition}[Finite-record maximality]
4131: \label{prop:publication-safety-maximality}
4132: Under the hypotheses of Theorem~\ref{thm:publication-safety-interface}, the
4133: constructed certificate is maximal with respect to the finite submitted record
4134: surface, in the dependency order isolated in
4135: Proposition~\ref{prop:publication-safety-dependency-normal-form}.  More
4136: precisely, let $c$ be an accepted load-bearing claim and let
4137: $\mathsf{Cert}_{\Gamma}(c)=(K,\ell,E,H,R,D,B)$ be the certificate constructed in
4138: that theorem.  If a stronger reading $c^{+}$ asserts any external end-to-end
4139: compiler correctness, current execution of unrecorded gates, proof-assistant
4140: correctness, current formal-source currency, dynamic artifact semantics, or venue
4141: acceptance, then either $c^{+}$ is not a distinct finite-record consequence of
4142: $\mathsf{Cert}_{\Gamma}(c)$, or its certificate must strictly enlarge one of
4143: $K,\ell,E,H,R,D,B$ by adding the corresponding claim kind, evidence surface,
4144: gate pass record, promotion or venue field, discovery-interface row, or
4145: command-run tuple.  In the latter case, if the enlargement is absent, a finite
4146: bad subrecord is returned by Theorem~\ref{thm:record-acceptance-exactness} or by
4147: one of the obstruction types of
4148: Theorem~\ref{thm:finite-certificate-obstruction-skeleton}.
4149: \end{proposition}
4150: 
4151: \begin{proof}
4152: The schema verifier used in Theorem~\ref{thm:publication-safety-interface} reads
4153: only the tuple \((K,\ell,E,H,R,D,B)\), the dispatch table $\Gamma$, and the cited
4154: submitted finite surfaces.  This is exactly the finite-coordinate determination
4155: boundary of Theorem~\ref{thm:finite-coordinate-determination-boundary}.  Hence two
4156: surrounding implementations, formal-source trees, artifact runs, or venue states
4157: that agree on those coordinates obtain the same verifier decision.  A purported
4158: stronger reading which changes none of the coordinates is therefore invisible to
4159: the certificate and cannot become a new accepted finite-record conclusion.
4160: 
4161: If the stronger reading is made load-bearing, it changes a coordinate.  External
4162: compiler, proof-assistant, formal-source-currency, or artifact-semantics claims
4163: add claim kinds and assigned gate records; fresh executions raise the evidence
4164: boundary to command-run data; route or venue assertions add promotion or venue
4165: surfaces; discovery-strengthening adds source-interface rows.  These are exactly
4166: the finite fields tested by the clauses of
4167: Definition~\ref{def:certificate-schema-acceptance-table}.  When the required
4168: field is missing, Theorem~\ref{thm:record-acceptance-exactness} supplies the
4169: failed acceptance clause, and
4170: Theorem~\ref{thm:finite-certificate-obstruction-skeleton} classifies the failure
4171: as a missing evidence surface, gate record, promotion field, discovery-interface
4172: row, or command-run escalation.\end{proof}
4173: 
4174: \begin{corollary}[Current-package bounded source-level audit instance]
4175: \label{cor:publication-safety-current-package-instance}
4176: For the current package, assume the support-surface condition later closed by
4177: Theorem~\ref{thm:final-support-surface-closure}: the exact supplemental
4178: \path{review_bundle/} directory is uploaded with the submission, or an
4179: archive-equivalent replacement with matching final digest records is supplied.
4180: Under that explicit availability condition, the finite pass-row components of
4181: the hypotheses of Theorem~\ref{thm:publication-safety-interface} are witnessed
4182: by the machine-readable evidence map of
4183: Proposition~\ref{prop:machine-readable-certificate-records}, the acceptance
4184: ledger of
4185: Proposition~\ref{prop:current-package-acceptance-certificate-ledger}, the
4186: assigned-gate boundary of
4187: Theorem~\ref{thm:assigned-gate-soundness-audit-boundary}, and the four
4188: path-complete case witnesses of
4189: Proposition~\ref{prop:path-complete-case-witnesses}.  Therefore the current
4190: package instantiates the construction theorem as a bounded software-audit
4191: certificate-construction and certificate-checking result for the finite records
4192: actually supplied, with the bounded schema-relative package certificate exactness
4193: isolated in Theorem~\ref{thm:current-package-certificate-exactness}.  It is not,
4194: without the separate extension coordinates listed in the boundary theorems, an
4195: instance of gate implementation soundness, Lean/BEDC rebuild correctness,
4196: artifact semantic validity, daemon behavior, upload, archive replacement,
4197: upload-time venue compliance, or venue acceptance.
4198: \end{corollary}
4199: 
4200: \begin{proof}
4201: Under the stated support-surface condition, each cited current-package result
4202: supplies one finite hypothesis of the abstract interface.  Proposition~\ref{prop:current-package-acceptance-certificate-ledger}
4203: lists the accepted rows and their evidence surfaces.  The assigned-gate boundary
4204: theorem gives the finite-record form of record-gate soundness and blocks an
4205: unrecorded implementation-soundness reading.  The machine-readable certificate
4206: records supply the schema mirror, pass-record rows, interface map, inventory,
4207: verifier script, positive verifier log, negative fixture, and negative verifier
4208: log when the exact review-bundle support surface is submitted with matching final
4209: digests.  The positive log records the invocation
4210: \path{python review_bundle/verify_certificate_records.py} and exit code
4211: \path{0}; the negative log records the invocation
4212: \path{python review_bundle/verify_certificate_records.py --negative-example review_bundle/negative_certificate_record_example.json}
4213: and the expected finite rejection \path{neg1: command-run fields incomplete}.
4214: The case-witness proposition supplies
4215: the finite case-study paths used by the accepted lesson rows.  Applying
4216: Theorem~\ref{thm:publication-safety-interface} to these records gives the stated
4217: bounded audit construction; Theorem~\ref{thm:current-package-certificate-exactness}
4218: is the row-frozen schema-relative exactness specialization.  The exclusions in
4219: the statement follow because none of the cited current-package records contains
4220: the extension coordinates required by
4221: Theorem~\ref{thm:finite-extension-budget-normal-form}.\end{proof}
4222: 
4223: \begin{corollary}[Finite-record boundary for external gate soundness]
4224: \label{cor:finite-record-gate-soundness-boundary}
4225: Under the hypotheses of the finite-record interface theorem, Theorem~\ref{thm:publication-safety-interface}, the
4226: publication-safety conclusion is invariant under replacement of the surrounding
4227: implementation by any other implementation with the same submitted acceptance
4228: entries, evidence surfaces, promotion fields, discovery-interface rows, and
4229: evidence-level boundaries.  Therefore the theorem cannot be used, without an
4230: additional submitted record-gate-soundness certificate, to infer external workflow
4231: correctness, current execution of unrecorded gates, Lean correctness, artifact
4232: semantic validity, or venue acceptance.  Conversely, any manuscript assertion
4233: that tries to promote the finite certificate constructor to one of those stronger
4234: implementation claims has a finite overclaim witness: either the required field
4235: is absent from \(\mathcal C(c)\), or the row's declared evidence level exceeds
4236: the cited certificate surface.
4237: \end{corollary}
4238: 
4239: \begin{proof}
4240: The constructor in the finite-record interface theorem, Theorem~\ref{thm:publication-safety-interface}, reads only the
4241: finite tuple \(\mathcal C(c)=(K,\ell,E,H,R,D,B)\).  Here \(K\) is the claim-kind
4242: set assigned by the dispatch table, \(\ell\) is the declared evidence level,
4243: \(E\) is the submitted evidence surface, \(H\) is the human-promotion field,
4244: \(R\) is the finite list of recorded pass rows, \(D\) is the pinned
4245: discovery-interface field when the claim has a discovery component, and \(B\) is
4246: the evidence boundary.  If another implementation supplies the same values for
4247: these fields, the same finite schema verifier receives the same input record and
4248: therefore gives the same certificate-construction conclusion by
4249: Proposition~\ref{prop:finite-certificate-schema-verifier} and
4250: the schema-relative finite-record exactness theorem, Theorem~\ref{thm:record-acceptance-exactness}.  No step of the proof inspected an
4251: unsubmitted daemon trace, reran a proof assistant, reran a finite-witness
4252: artifact, or queried a venue form after the recorded venue check; those facts are
4253: not coordinates of \(\mathcal C(c)\).
4254: 
4255: Now suppose the manuscript asserted one of the stronger implementation claims
4256: without adding a new accepted record.  External workflow correctness and current
4257: execution of unrecorded gates require pass data not present in \(R\).  Lean
4258: correctness requires a formal-source certificate rather than the bounded
4259: contextual rows allowed by Proposition~\ref{prop:formal-source-gate-instantiation}.
4260: Artifact semantic validity requires a stronger artifact row than the bounded
4261: manifest records of Theorem~\ref{thm:finite-witness-manifest-record-interface}.
4262: Venue acceptance requires a venue decision rather than the submission-pack
4263: readiness row of Proposition~\ref{audit:venue-submission-pack-gate}.  In each
4264: case, either the needed finite field is missing or the row is read at a stronger
4265: evidence level than its certificate permits.  The obstruction is therefore a
4266: finite subrecord of the kind classified by
4267: Theorem~\ref{thm:finite-certificate-obstruction-skeleton} and
4268: Theorem~\ref{thm:evidence-level-overclaim-obstruction}.\end{proof}
4269: 
4270: \begin{theorem}[Boundary extension criterion for implementation-soundness readings]
4271: \label{thm:implementation-soundness-extension-criterion}
4272: Fix the dispatch table $\Gamma$ and the finite certificate schema of
4273: Proposition~\ref{prop:finite-certificate-schema-verifier}.  A reading of a
4274: load-bearing package claim as an implementation-soundness claim is accepted by
4275: the same publication-safety interface if and only if the submitted finite record
4276: surface is enlarged by an implementation-soundness extension record
4277: \[
4278: \mathsf{Impl}(c)=(K^{\mathrm{impl}},E^{\mathrm{impl}},R^{\mathrm{impl}},B^{\mathrm{impl}})
4279: \]
4280: with the following properties.
4281: \begin{enumerate}
4282: \item $K^{\mathrm{impl}}$ contains the additional implementation claim kind or
4283: claim-kind component being asserted, rather than merely reusing the original
4284: finite-record claim kind of $c$.
4285: \item $E^{\mathrm{impl}}$ is a submitted finite evidence surface naming the
4286: implementation object being certified: for example the relevant source path,
4287: version or commit, configuration, executable verifier, and implementation-log
4288: surface.
4289: \item $R^{\mathrm{impl}}$ contains a pass record, with evidence pointer inside
4290: $E^{\mathrm{impl}}$, for every gate assigned by $\Gamma$ to the enlarged claim
4291: kind $K\cup K^{\mathrm{impl}}$.
4292: \item $B^{\mathrm{impl}}$ declares the evidence level of the implementation
4293: claim and contains the command-run tuple whenever the implementation claim is
4294: asserted at command-run strength.
4295: \end{enumerate}
4296: When such an extension record is present, the finite certificate constructor of
4297: Theorem~\ref{thm:publication-safety-interface} accepts the enlarged claim exactly
4298: at the evidence level declared by $B^{\mathrm{impl}}$.  When it is absent, every
4299: implementation-soundness reading of the same row has a finite obstruction of one
4300: of the five kinds in Theorem~\ref{thm:finite-certificate-obstruction-skeleton}.
4301: The current package supplies no record of the displayed form
4302: $\mathsf{Impl}(c)$ for the package verifiers or gates.  Thus the theorem is a
4303: future-facing exact criterion rather than an implementation-soundness result for
4304: this submission: it says precisely what finite record must be added before a
4305: later package may make that stronger claim.
4306: \end{theorem}
4307: 
4308: \begin{proof}
4309: First suppose the extension record is present.  Replace the original row for
4310: $c$ by the enlarged row whose claim-kind coordinate is $K\cup K^{\mathrm{impl}}$,
4311: whose evidence surface is $E\cup E^{\mathrm{impl}}$, whose pass-record field is
4312: $R\cup R^{\mathrm{impl}}$, and whose boundary field is the original boundary
4313: together with $B^{\mathrm{impl}}$.  The first condition makes the stronger
4314: reading visible to the dispatch table rather than hiding it in prose.  The second
4315: condition supplies a finite submitted surface for the implementation object.  The
4316: third condition supplies every gate record required by $\Gamma$ for the enlarged
4317: claim-kind set, and the fourth condition prevents a path-verified implementation
4318: record from being read as a command-run implementation certificate without the
4319: command, source-state, environment, exit-code, and log-path fields.  Therefore
4320: the tuple has exactly the fields checked by
4321: Proposition~\ref{prop:finite-certificate-schema-verifier}.  Applying
4322: Theorem~\ref{thm:publication-safety-interface} to the enlarged row constructs an
4323: accepted finite certificate for the implementation-soundness reading, and the
4324: accepted strength is precisely the declared evidence level because the schema
4325: reads no unsubmitted evidence-level coordinate.
4326: 
4327: Conversely, suppose a manuscript row is read as an implementation-soundness claim
4328: but no extension record with the four displayed properties is submitted.  If the
4329: additional implementation claim kind is not present in the claim-kind coordinate,
4330: then the stronger reading has no assigned implementation gate and is an
4331: unsupported escalation of the original row.  If the kind is present but
4332: $E^{\mathrm{impl}}$ is absent, the claim lacks a submitted evidence surface for
4333: the implementation object, giving obstruction type~1 of
4334: Theorem~\ref{thm:finite-certificate-obstruction-skeleton}.  If the evidence
4335: surface is present but some gate assigned by $\Gamma$ has no corresponding pass
4336: record in $R^{\mathrm{impl}}$, the bad subrecord is type~2.  If the
4337: implementation reading crosses an intake, source, route, or venue boundary with
4338: no required promotion or decision field, the bad subrecord is type~3.  If the
4339: implementation reading is also a discovery or classifier-shift reading without
4340: the pinned discovery-interface rows, the bad subrecord is type~4.  Finally, if
4341: the row asserts command-run implementation strength without the command-run tuple
4342: inside $B^{\mathrm{impl}}$, the bad subrecord is type~5.  These cases exhaust the
4343: finite schema fields by the obstruction-skeleton theorem, so absence of the
4344: extension record is exactly absence of finite support for the stronger reading.
4345: Inspecting Table~\ref{tab:current-certificate-ledger} and
4346: Proposition~\ref{prop:machine-readable-certificate-records}, the current package
4347: records path-verified certificate rows $a_1,\ldots,a_7$ and command-boundary
4348: inventory rows for bounded verifier invocations, but it does not add a new
4349: implementation claim kind, implementation evidence surface, implementation gate
4350: pass family, and implementation boundary tuple as a single
4351: $\mathsf{Impl}(c)$ extension record.  Therefore this theorem is not instantiated
4352: as an implementation-soundness theorem here; it remains an exact finite-record
4353: extension test for a later package.
4354: \end{proof}
4355: 
4356: \begin{corollary}[Submitted gate-implementation contract dichotomy]
4357: \label{cor:submitted-gate-implementation-contract-dichotomy}
4358: Fix the package ledger $L$, support surface $S$, and dispatch table $\Gamma$ of
4359: the publication-safety interface.  A claim that the actual gate-producing
4360: daemons or human workflow of the package satisfy record-gate soundness is
4361: accepted by the theorem chain of this paper only in the following submitted-form
4362: case: the package includes a finite implementation contract
4363: \[
4364: \mathsf{GateImpl}(P)=(I,E_I,R_I,B_I)
4365: \]
4366: where $I$ names the gate-producing implementations and human boundary
4367: procedures, $E_I\subseteq S$ names their source, configuration, replay inputs,
4368: and produced-row surfaces, $R_I$ contains a submitted pass record for every
4369: implementation or human-boundary gate assigned by $\Gamma$ to that stronger
4370: claim, and $B_I$ contains the command-run tuple whenever the contract is read at
4371: command-run strength.  If no such finite tuple is submitted, then every system
4372: soundness reading of Theorem~\ref{thm:publication-safety-interface} is rejected:
4373: the theorem remains strictly conditional on the submitted gate-passage records
4374: in $L$ and asserts no independent soundness of the daemons, scripts, or human
4375: workflow that produced those rows.
4376: \end{corollary}
4377: 
4378: \begin{proof}
4379: The claim that the actual daemons, scripts, or human workflow satisfy
4380: record-gate soundness is an implementation-soundness reading of the finite
4381: record row.  Apply Theorem~\ref{thm:implementation-soundness-extension-criterion}
4382: with $K^{\mathrm{impl}}$ equal to the additional implementation component,
4383: $E^{\mathrm{impl}}=E_I$, $R^{\mathrm{impl}}=R_I$, and
4384: $B^{\mathrm{impl}}=B_I$.  If the displayed contract is present, the enlarged row
4385: has an explicit implementation kind, a submitted evidence surface for the
4386: implementation objects and human boundary procedures, all assigned pass rows,
4387: and the declared evidence-level boundary.  The finite certificate constructor
4388: therefore accepts exactly that enlarged finite-record claim and no stronger one.
4389: 
4390: Conversely, suppose the manuscript is read as asserting such implementation
4391: soundness while no tuple $\mathsf{GateImpl}(P)$ is submitted.  If $I$ or the
4392: corresponding implementation claim kind is missing, the stronger reading is
4393: invisible to $\Gamma$; if $E_I$ is missing, there is no submitted implementation
4394: surface; if $R_I$ is missing, some assigned implementation or human-boundary gate
4395: has no submitted pass row; and if $B_I$ is missing at command-run strength, the
4396: evidence-level boundary is incomplete.  These are exactly the finite obstruction
4397: cases classified by Theorem~\ref{thm:finite-certificate-obstruction-skeleton}
4398: and by Theorem~\ref{thm:implementation-soundness-extension-criterion}.  Hence,
4399: without the displayed tuple, the only remaining hypothesis available to
4400: Theorem~\ref{thm:publication-safety-interface} is the conditional finite
4401: predicate $\mathsf{RGS}_{S}(P,L,\Gamma)$ over the submitted gate-passage records;
4402: no independent implementation-level soundness follows.
4403: \end{proof}
4404: 
4405: \begin{theorem}[External-interface projection and no-free-implementation upgrade]
4406: \label{thm:external-interface-projection-no-free-upgrade}
4407: Fix a package $P$, submitted surface $S$, acceptance ledger $L$, and dispatch
4408: table $\Gamma$.  Let $\pi(P,S,L,\Gamma)$ be the finite record projection that
4409: retains only the fields read by the certificate schema: claim kinds, evidence
4410: levels, submitted evidence pointers, human-promotion fields, assigned passing
4411: gate rows, discovery-interface rows, and command-boundary tuples.  Then the
4412: following are equivalent for the publication-safety conclusion of
4413: Theorem~\ref{thm:publication-safety-interface}.
4414: \begin{enumerate}
4415: \item The theorem constructs the accepted publication certificates for the
4416: load-bearing rows of $L$.
4417: \item The projected finite record $\pi(P,S,L,\Gamma)$ satisfies
4418: $\mathsf{RGS}_{S}(P,L,\Gamma)$ and the remaining finite hypotheses of
4419: Theorem~\ref{thm:publication-safety-interface}.
4420: \end{enumerate}
4421: Consequently any two gate-producing implementations, daemon histories, or human
4422: workflows with the same projection $\pi(P,S,L,\Gamma)$ are indistinguishable by
4423: the theorem: they yield the same accepted certificates and the same finite bad
4424: subrecords.  A stronger reading asserting independent soundness of the gates,
4425: scripts, daemons, or human workflow is accepted if and only if the projection is
4426: strictly enlarged by an implementation-soundness extension record of the form
4427: specified in Theorem~\ref{thm:implementation-soundness-extension-criterion}; in
4428: particular, when a command-run implementation reading is asserted, that extension
4429: must include command, source state, environment, exit code, and log path.  Without
4430: that enlargement the stronger implementation reading is rejected while the
4431: finite-record interface certificate, if otherwise valid, remains accepted at its
4432: declared finite evidence level.
4433: \end{theorem}
4434: 
4435: \begin{proof}
4436: The verifier predicates $P_1,\ldots,P_6$ in the certificate schema read exactly
4437: the fields retained by $\pi$: the claim-kind coordinate, the evidence-level and
4438: evidence-surface coordinates, the assigned gate rows, the human-promotion field,
4439: the discovery-interface field, and the command-boundary field.  Therefore
4440: replacing $(P,S,L,\Gamma)$ by another package with the same projection gives the
4441: same input to Proposition~\ref{prop:finite-certificate-schema-verifier}.  The
4442: schema-relative exactness theorem, Theorem~\ref{thm:record-acceptance-exactness},
4443: then gives the same acceptance value and the same rejected bad subrecord for each
4444: load-bearing row.  Proposition~\ref{prop:record-gate-soundness-external-interface-exactness}
4445: identifies this projected record condition with the finite predicate
4446: $\mathsf{RGS}_{S}(P,L,\Gamma)$ used by
4447: Theorem~\ref{thm:publication-safety-interface}.  Thus the theorem's certificate
4448: construction depends on the package only through $\pi$ and the explicitly stated
4449: finite hypotheses, proving the equivalence and the indistinguishability claim.
4450: 
4451: It remains to characterize stronger implementation readings.  Such a reading
4452: adds an implementation claim-kind component or raises the evidence level of an
4453: existing gate row beyond the submitted row-and-pointer record.  By
4454: Theorem~\ref{thm:implementation-soundness-extension-criterion}, acceptance of
4455: that stronger reading is equivalent to adding the four implementation extension
4456: coordinates $K^{\mathrm{impl}}$, $E^{\mathrm{impl}}$, $R^{\mathrm{impl}}$, and
4457: $B^{\mathrm{impl}}$; at command-run strength the fourth coordinate contains the
4458: command, source state, environment, exit code, and log path.  If the extension is
4459: present, the enlarged projection is checked by the same schema and the stronger
4460: finite-record claim is accepted at its declared evidence level.  If the extension
4461: is absent, one of the finite coordinates required by the schema is missing, so
4462: Theorem~\ref{thm:finite-certificate-obstruction-skeleton} supplies the finite bad
4463: subrecord.  The original finite-record certificate is unaffected because its
4464: projection has not changed.
4465: \end{proof}
